# Network phase N2: ping a real peer (QEMU's built-in gateway), and the
# address survives a reboot.
@net
@disk
$ net static 10.0.2.15/24 10.0.2.2
address 10.0.2.15/24, gateway 10.0.2.2 (saved)
$ cat /system/net
static 10.0.2.15/24 10.0.2.2
@reboot
~ \[net\]  e1000 \(Intel 82540EM\) 52:54:00:be:e0:01, link up
~ \[net\]  address 10\.0\.2\.15/24 \(static\), gateway 10\.0\.2\.2
$ ping -c 3 10.0.2.2
PING 10.0.2.2: 32 data bytes
~ ^32 bytes from 10\.0\.2\.2: seq=1 ttl=\d+ time=[\d.]+ ms$
~ ^32 bytes from 10\.0\.2\.2: seq=2 ttl=\d+ time=[\d.]+ ms$
~ ^32 bytes from 10\.0\.2\.2: seq=3 ttl=\d+ time=[\d.]+ ms$
3 sent, 3 received, 0% loss
~ ^round trip min/avg/max = [\d.]+ ms/[\d.]+ ms/[\d.]+ ms$
$ arp
~ ^10\.0\.2\.2\s+52:55:0a:00:02:02  confirmed \d+ s ago$
$ net off
IP address removed (saved)
@reboot
!~ \[net\]  address
$ net
address: none (net dhcp, or net static IP/PREFIX [GATEWAY])
